---
name: scroll-fade
type: registry:lib
title: Scroll Fade
description: Utilities for scroll-aware fading at the edges of overflow containers.
dependencies:
  - shadcn-svelte
files:
  - path: registry/items/utilities/scroll-fade/scroll-fade.ts
    type: registry:lib
    target: "~/lib/scroll-fade.ts"
---

These class helpers use the utilities shipped in `shadcn-svelte/tailwind.css`. The design system [Styles](/utilities/styles) already imports them. In other projects, add this once to your global CSS:

```css
@import "tailwindcss";
@import "shadcn-svelte/tailwind.css";
```

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { scrollFadeClass } from "~/lib/scroll-fade";
</script>

<div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl border">
  <div class={`${scrollFadeClass} max-h-48`}>
    <!-- Scrollable content -->
  </div>
</div>
```

Put borders and backgrounds on an outer wrapper so the mask only fades the content. Class helpers include overflow behavior; direct CSS classes can also be used in markup.

| Class | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `scroll-fade` / `scroll-fade-y` | Fade vertical edges |
| `scroll-fade-x` | Fade horizontal edges |
| `scroll-fade-t`, `-b`, `-l`, `-r` | Fade a physical edge |
| `scroll-fade-s`, `-e` | Fade logical inline edges, including RTL |
| `scroll-fade-<n>` | Set fade depth using the spacing scale |
| `scroll-fade-none` | Disable fading |

Exports include `scrollFadeClass`, `scrollFadeYClass`, `scrollFadeXClass`, `scrollFadeEdgeClasses`, `scrollFadeSizeClasses`, and `scrollFadeNoneClass`. Helpers contain complete literal class names that Tailwind can detect. Browsers with scroll timelines reveal fades as content scrolls; browsers without them use static edge masks.

[Upstream Scroll Fade documentation](https://www.shadcn-svelte.com/docs/utils/scroll-fade).
